#!/bin/sh
set -eu

# Rayburst is a Tauri v2 (WebKitGTK) app, unpacked by apply_extra from
# upstream's AppImage into /app/extra/rayburst, the linuxdeploy AppDir kept
# whole. This starts it the way the AppImage itself does, through the AppDir's
# AppRun, so the app runs on the GTK 3 and WebKitGTK 4.1 it bundles rather
# than on anything from the runtime (why: the comment at the top of the
# manifest). What AppRun does, in order:
#
#   AppRun          (bash) sources apprun-hooks/linuxdeploy-plugin-gtk.sh, which
#                   points GTK_PATH, GTK_DATA_PREFIX, GIO_MODULE_DIR,
#                   GSETTINGS_SCHEMA_DIR, GDK_PIXBUF_MODULE_FILE and
#                   XDG_DATA_DIRS into the AppDir and sets GTK_THEME to
#                   Adwaita, light or dark from the portal's color-scheme.
#                   It reads that with dbus-send, then gsettings; both are in
#                   the runtime, and if either fails the hook falls back to
#                   light (`|| printf ''`), so a failure is harmless.
#   AppRun.wrapped  (ELF) puts the AppDir's usr/lib on LD_LIBRARY_PATH and
#                   usr/bin on PATH, changes into usr/ and runs the Exec= of the
#                   AppDir's desktop entry. Changing into usr/ is load-bearing:
#                   the bundled libwebkit2gtk looks for WebKitWebProcess and
#                   WebKitNetworkProcess at ././/lib/x86_64-linux-gnu/webkit2gtk-4.1,
#                   relative to the working directory.
#
# Starting usr/bin/rayburst directly would load the bundled libraries through
# its $ORIGIN/../lib RUNPATH, but without the hook's variables GTK and GIO
# would look for their modules, schemas and pixbuf loaders in the runtime's
# paths, and without the cd WebKit's helpers would not be found.
#
# The engine sidecar (usr/bin/aria2-next) and the resource tree it reads
# (usr/lib/Rayburst) are where AppRun expects them. Flatpak redirects the XDG
# base dirs, so the task database, the settings and the engine's session state
# stay inside the sandbox.

# No WebKitGTK rendering variables here. The app sets them itself before GTK
# starts (src-tauri/src/webkit.rs): it removes WEBKIT_DISABLE_DMABUF_RENDERER,
# forces the DMA-BUF renderer in shared-memory mode and paints on the CPU
# unless its own "hardware rendering" preference is on, so anything exported
# here is overwritten. It leaves WEBKIT_DISABLE_COMPOSITING_MODE alone; that
# stays unset too, because the window is `transparent: true` and turning
# compositing off has left it see-through before (on the GNOME 51 runtime's
# WebKitGTK). These now act on the WebKitGTK inside the AppImage, which is the
# one upstream's own AppImage users run.

# No LD_PRELOAD getpid shim here, unlike com.getupnote.UpNote, even though the
# ksni tray does claim org.kde.StatusNotifierItem-<getpid()>-1 (granted as -2-1
# in the manifest). The shim would leak into the aria2-next engine this process
# spawns, and getpid shims have broken other apps here (docs/reference/
# incidents.md); the PID-2 contention is the smaller risk until upstream skips
# the well-known name in a sandbox. Every step from here to the app is an exec,
# so the app keeps the PID this wrapper started with.

# AppRun is the AppImage format's fixed entry point; the launcher's own name
# comes from the AppDir's desktop entry, which apply_extra checked.
exec /app/extra/rayburst/AppRun "$@"
